---
import { DEFAULT_MODEL, optimalSwitchDistance, switchDistance } from "../model/elevator";
import type { AdvancedModel } from "../model/elevator";
import { projectToShaftPercent, shaftDomain } from "../scripts/elevator-animation";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const shaftExtent = shaftDomain(DEFAULT_MODEL);
const targetPercent = projectToShaftPercent(DEFAULT_MODEL.H, shaftExtent);
const switchPercent = projectToShaftPercent(switchDistance(DEFAULT_MODEL, 50), shaftExtent);

const ASYMMETRIC_SAMPLE_MODELS: { label: string; model: AdvancedModel }[] = [
  { label: "Weaker braking — switch earlier", model: { H: 10, a: 2, b: 1 } },
  { label: "Balanced — switch halfway", model: { H: 10, a: 1.5, b: 1.5 } },
  { label: "Stronger braking — switch later", model: { H: 10, a: 1, b: 2 } },
];
const asymmetricShafts = ASYMMETRIC_SAMPLE_MODELS.map(({ label, model }) => {
  const extent = shaftDomain(model);
  return {
    label,
    model,
    targetPercent: projectToShaftPercent(model.H, extent),
    switchPercent: projectToShaftPercent(optimalSwitchDistance(model), extent),
  };
});
---

<Layout title="Why? — the idea behind the puzzle" current="principle">
  <main>
    <section aria-labelledby="principle-heading">
      <h1 id="principle-heading">Why?</h1>
      <p data-testid="principle-intro">
        Every time an elevator answers a call, it solves this same problem: get to the right floor, and be completely
        still when the doors open. Nobody riding it thinks of this as an optimisation problem — it just happens,
        smoothly, every time. This page is about what's actually being solved underneath that smoothness, and why the
        fastest possible journey isn't what you might guess at first.
      </p>

      <div class="principle-layout">
        <figure class="principle-visual" data-testid="principle-visual">
          <div class="shaft-col">
            <div class="shaft" aria-hidden="true">
              <div
                data-testid="principle-target-marker"
                class="marker marker-target"
                style={`bottom: ${targetPercent}%;`}
              >
              </div>
              <div
                data-testid="principle-switch-marker"
                class="marker marker-fastest-valid"
                style={`bottom: ${switchPercent}%;`}
              >
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            The switch point sits exactly halfway between the start and the target — see "Why the answer is exactly
            halfway" below.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="principle-content">
          <section aria-labelledby="arrival-heading">
            <h2 id="arrival-heading">Reaching the floor isn't the same as arriving</h2>
            <p>
              On the Play page, a run that lands exactly on the target floor while still moving isn't a success — the
              elevator has to be at rest, not just in the right place, when it gets there. Two things are always
              changing during the trip: where the elevator is, and how fast it's moving. Reaching the target position
              while still moving means it's about to overshoot; stopping too soon means being at rest short of the
              target. Being exactly at the target with exactly zero speed is the one state that counts as arrival.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="switch-heading">
            <h2 id="switch-heading">Why full power one way, then full power the other, is fastest</h2>
            <p>
              If the only goal were speed, wasting any time not accelerating would be time never recovered. But
              arrival also requires the elevator to be at rest at the target, so it can't accelerate the whole way —
              whatever speed it builds up has to be shed again before it gets there, by braking just as hard. Easing
              off early, coasting, or braking gently instead of fully all take longer to reach the same state. So the
              fastest possible journey is: accelerate at the maximum rate for as long as possible, then switch to
              braking at the maximum rate, timed so the elevator arrives at the target exactly as its speed reaches
              zero. There's exactly one moment to make that switch — switch earlier and the elevator stops short;
              switch later and it's still moving when it reaches the target.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="halfway-heading">
            <h2 id="halfway-heading">Why the answer is exactly halfway</h2>
            <p>
              For this elevator, the maximum acceleration and the maximum braking are equally strong. Speeding up and
              slowing down are then exact mirror images of each other: reaching a given speed by accelerating takes
              exactly as much distance as shedding that same speed by braking. So the switch has to happen exactly
              halfway between the start and the target — the same {"50%"} the Play page's hint reveals. With this model's
              numbers ({DEFAULT_MODEL.H} m, {DEFAULT_MODEL.a} m/s²), that switch happens at 5 m, reaching a top speed
              of about 3.87 m/s, for a fastest possible journey of about 5.16 s.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="asymmetric-heading">
            <h2 id="asymmetric-heading">WHAT IF THE RULES AREN'T BALANCED?</h2>
            <p>
              The explanation above is actually the balanced special case: Beginner always sets the acceleration
              and braking limits equal, so speeding up and slowing down are perfect mirror images and the switch
              falls exactly halfway. Advanced lets you break that assumption.
            </p>
            <p>
              Advanced defines two idealised effective limits instead of one: <strong>a</strong>, how quickly the elevator can speed up, and <strong>b</strong>, how quickly it can slow down. Neither is a real
              force, mass, motor power, or gravity value; they're simplified limits on motion, same as Beginner's
              single acceleration, just no longer required to match each other.
            </p>
            <p>
              If braking is weaker than acceleration, the elevator can't shed speed as quickly, so it has to start
              braking earlier to still stop exactly on target. If braking is stronger, it can accelerate for
              longer and brake later. When the two limits are equal, the switch falls exactly halfway, just like
              Beginner.
            </p>
            <p>
              The same reasoning as before makes this exact. At a switch distance s, accelerating from rest
              reaches a speed of v*² = 2as. Stopping in the remaining distance then requires
              H − s = v*²/(2b) = as/b. Solving for the switch distance gives s* = Hb/(a+b), or as a percentage of
              the journey, p* = 100b/(a+b) — exactly what Play's Advanced Reveal computes.
            </p>
            <p>
              Changing H moves the physical switch distance and the total journey time, but it does not change the optimal percentage: H cancels out of p* = 100b/(a+b) completely. This is the value recalculated when you change the rules in Play.
            </p>

            <div class="mini-shaft-row">
              {
                asymmetricShafts.map(({ label, model, targetPercent: miniTargetPercent, switchPercent: miniSwitchPercent }) => (
                  <figure class="mini-shaft-figure">
                    <div class="shaft mini-shaft" data-testid="mini-shaft" data-sample-model={JSON.stringify(model)}>
                      <div class="marker marker-target" style={`bottom: ${miniTargetPercent}%;`}></div>
                      <div
                        data-testid="mini-shaft-switch-marker"
                        class="marker marker-fastest-valid"
                        style={`bottom: ${miniSwitchPercent}%;`}
                      >
                      </div>
                    </div>
                    <figcaption>{label}</figcaption>
                  </figure>
                ))
              }
            </div>
          </section>

          <details data-testid="formal-model">
            <summary>For the mathematically curious</summary>
            <p>Stated formally, in the language of control theory:</p>
            <pre>ẋ = v
v̇ = u,  |u| ≤ a
x(0) = 0, v(0) = 0
x(T) = H, v(T) = 0
minimise T</pre>
            <p>
              This is a <em>double integrator</em> — position is the double integral of a bounded control input. The
              result that the fastest control switches at most once, from full acceleration to full braking, is a
              classical result in optimal control theory, provable using Pontryagin's Minimum Principle. Because the
              optimal <em>u(t)</em> only ever takes its extreme values (`+a` or `-a`), this kind of solution is called
              <em>bang-bang control</em>. Analysing it directly in the position/velocity state-space, sometimes called
              the phase plane, makes the single-switch structure visible geometrically as well as algebraically. See
              References at the end of this section for the sources this page's model was checked against.
            </p>
            <h3 id="references-heading">References</h3>
            <ul data-testid="principle-references">
              <li>
                <a href="http://underactuated.mit.edu/dp.html">
                  Tedrake, Russ. <cite>Underactuated Robotics</cite>, Chapter 7: "Dynamic Programming" (MIT course
                  notes).
                </a>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07851">
                  Burachik, Caldwell, Kaya, Moursi. "Best Approximation Optimal Control for Infeasible Double
                  Integrator and Douglas–Rachford Algorithm." arXiv:2602.07851.
                </a>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.03192">
                  Romano, Curti. "Analytic Solution of the Time-Optimal Control of a Double Integrator from an
                  Arbitrary State to the State-space Origin." arXiv:1909.03192.
                </a>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </details>

          <section aria-labelledby="real-heading">
            <h2 id="real-heading">How this compares to a real elevator</h2>
            <p>
              This page and the Play interface both use a deliberately simplified model: a single point that speeds
              up and slows down at a constant rate, with no mass, motor behaviour, cabling, ride comfort, or safety
              systems taken into account. A real elevator's motor has torque and speed limits that change with load,
              its acceleration is smoothed for passenger comfort rather than snapping instantly to a maximum, and
              safety systems constrain how it behaves near floors. The idealised model here isn't a claim about how
              real elevator controllers work — it's a simplified version of the same underlying puzzle, chosen
              because it has an exact, checkable answer.
            </p>
          </section>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>
